\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.0.1}{\ignorespaces The running tree \(T\) with the merge overlay. Below each node: \(\depth  \) (root at~\(0\)), \(\parent  \), \(\size  \) = subtree node count (\(\parent  [1]=0\) sentinel). Above each node: the colour \(c\) and the \texttt  {big} pointer -- the child with the largest subtree (the heavy child the sack keeps; "\(-\)'' on a leaf). Sanity checks reused everywhere: \(\size  (3)=5>\size  (2)=4\) so \(\texttt  {big}[1]=3\); inside node~2, \(\size  (5)=2> \size  (4)=1\) so \(\texttt  {big}[2]=5\); colour~1 appears on \(\{1,3,6,9\}\) and is the unique majority in the whole tree (\(\S  \ref {sec:c28-sack}\)), while colours~2 and~3 each appear three times -- a tie that makes node~9's answer non-obvious. Every DSU merge, potential, set-merge and sack schedule in 17.1--17.5 re-uses exactly these rows.}}{1012}{figure.caption.176}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c28-tree}{{17.0.1}{1012}{The running tree \(T\) with the merge overlay. Below each node: \(\depth \) (root at~\(0\)), \(\parent \), \(\size \) = subtree node count (\(\parent [1]=0\) sentinel). Above each node: the colour \(c\) and the \code {big} pointer -- the child with the largest subtree (the heavy child the sack keeps; "\(-\)'' on a leaf). Sanity checks reused everywhere: \(\size (3)=5>\size (2)=4\) so \(\code {big}[1]=3\); inside node~2, \(\size (5)=2> \size (4)=1\) so \(\code {big}[2]=5\); colour~1 appears on \(\{1,3,6,9\}\) and is the unique majority in the whole tree (\(\secref {c28-sack}\)), while colours~2 and~3 each appear three times -- a tie that makes node~9's answer non-obvious. Every DSU merge, potential, set-merge and sack schedule in 17.1--17.5 re-uses exactly these rows}{figure.caption.176}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.0.2}{\ignorespaces The chapter's six-state taxonomy, read top to bottom as an escalation. Rows 1--2 are the bare DSU; row~3 is the DSU that also \emph  {knows which side of a bipartition} each node is on; row~4 is the DSU that can \emph  {travel back in time}; rows~5--6 are the two subtree-merging machines (one returns per-node maps, one keeps a single global bag). Every solved problem sits on exactly one row: CSES~1666 on rows~1--2, CF~1594D on row~3, CSES~1139 on row~5, CF~600E on row~6, the offline dynamic-connectivity worked example on row~4, and the Summit CF~1009F spanning rows~5--6.}}{1013}{figure.caption.177}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c28-taxonomy}{{17.0.2}{1013}{The chapter's six-state taxonomy, read top to bottom as an escalation. Rows 1--2 are the bare DSU; row~3 is the DSU that also \emph {knows which side of a bipartition} each node is on; row~4 is the DSU that can \emph {travel back in time}; rows~5--6 are the two subtree-merging machines (one returns per-node maps, one keeps a single global bag). Every solved problem sits on exactly one row: CSES~1666 on rows~1--2, CF~1594D on row~3, CSES~1139 on row~5, CF~600E on row~6, the offline dynamic-connectivity worked example on row~4, and the Summit CF~1009F spanning rows~5--6}{figure.caption.177}{}}
