\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.2.1}{\ignorespaces The rollback invariant in miniature (not \(T\) -- a generic step). A union overwrites exactly two cells, \(\texttt  {parent}[b]\) and \(\texttt  {sz}[a]\); the record \((b,a,\text  {old}\ \texttt  {sz}[a])\) captures both, so rewinding is a two-field restore, leaving every other pointer and the \(\ensuremath  {O(\log n)}\) depth bound untouched. With path compression the same union would have silently rewritten a whole search path -- unrecoverable from a single record, which is why compression is forbidden here. The full add/undo run on \(T\)'s nine edges is the table below.}}{1028}{figure.caption.180}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c28-rollback}{{17.2.1}{1028}{The rollback invariant in miniature (not \(T\) -- a generic step). A union overwrites exactly two cells, \(\code {parent}[b]\) and \(\code {sz}[a]\); the record \((b,a,\text {old}\ \code {sz}[a])\) captures both, so rewinding is a two-field restore, leaving every other pointer and the \(\bigO {\log n}\) depth bound untouched. With path compression the same union would have silently rewritten a whole search path -- unrecoverable from a single record, which is why compression is forbidden here. The full add/undo run on \(T\)'s nine edges is the table below}{figure.caption.180}{}}
